      "documentConcept": "Fall prevention, home safety and mobility aids in PD",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:5e8b129ad8e33268d10a37c9eb401a2bc93d0e37d28f65498579c7c9bb42ae8e",
      "text": "PD fall prevention combines assessment of gait, balance and orthostatic symptoms with individualized exercise, home-safety assessment and mobility-aid selection. Exercise evidence is strongest for mild-to-moderate PD; improvements in balance or freezing do not invariably translate into fewer falls. Home guidance emphasizes clear routes, lighting, grab bars, seated bathing and emergency communication. Rollator findings are promising but observational, and walking aids can worsen some gait outcomes. Professional assessment should account for cognition, device handling and the home environment. User reports provide practical questions rather than efficacy estimates.",
      "sources": [
        "https://fallpreventionfoundation.org/2026/08/10/parkinsons-disease-and-home-safety-room-by-room-strategies-to-prevent-falls-and-maintain-independence/",
